---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import PlanningCTA from '../components/PlanningCTA.astro';
import { images } from '../data/assets';
const breadcrumbs=[{label:'How It Works',href:'/how-it-works/'}];
const stages=[
  ['01','Define the Trip','We start with the people, purpose, dates, source markets, activity level, comfort, approximate budget, and the result the organizer wants.'],
  ['02','Shape the Plan','We compare realistic destinations and formats, then develop route, lodging, transport, activities, operators, and support around the group.'],
  ['03','Prepare to Launch','We organize the information participants need to understand the trip, including inclusions, pace, rooming, deadlines, and readiness requirements.'],
  ['04','Confirm the Group','Traveler commitments, supplier terms, deposits, rooming, and important trip assumptions are brought into a confirmed operating picture.'],
  ['05','Prepare to Travel','Participants receive practical guidance around documents, insurance, equipment, fitness, certifications, arrivals, and final travel details.'],
  ['06','Travel Supported','The final itinerary defines who is supporting each part of the trip and how travelers reach help when plans change or questions arise.']
];
---
<BaseLayout title="How Group Travel Planning Works | Talon Expeditions" description="How Talon Expeditions moves a group trip from early idea through itinerary design, participant launch, confirmation, traveler preparation, and supported departure." image={images.planning} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}>
  <section class="page-hero" style={`background-image:url('${images.planning}')`}><div class="shell"><Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbs}/><p class="eyebrow">How group travel planning works</p><h1 class="section-title">Make the important decisions before they become expensive decisions.</h1><p>Talon Expeditions uses a staged planning process that starts with the group and progressively reduces uncertainty. The process is designed to help organizers avoid locking in disconnected bookings before the trip is viable.</p></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="shell definition-grid"><div class="definition-sticky"><p class="eyebrow">The operating model</p><h2>A group trip is a sequence of decisions, not a shopping cart.</h2></div><div class="definition-body"><div class="answer-panel"><h2>How does Talon Expeditions plan a group trip?</h2><p>We move from group definition to trip design, supplier coordination, participant launch, confirmation, preparation, and final travel support. At each stage, the goal is to answer the questions that affect the next commitment before the organizer or travelers are asked to make it.</p></div><p>Most planning problems are easier to solve early. If the group is likely to include beginners, that should influence the destination before a technical route is selected. If travelers will arrive from several cities, that should influence the first night before airport transfers are booked. If the trip needs a minimum group size, that should be understood before the organizer presents a fixed price as if nothing can change.</p><p>The process below is intentionally practical. Some simple trips move through it quickly. More complex or specialist journeys may require several rounds of supplier work and group decisions.</p></div></div></section>
  <section class="section soft-section"><div class="shell"><div class="specialty-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Six planning stages</p><h2 class="section-title">From concept to departure.</h2></div><p class="section-copy">The stages create clear handoffs between strategy, itinerary design, participant communication, confirmation, and readiness.</p></div><div class="donor-grid">{stages.map(([n,t,c])=><div class="donor-card"><span class="card-index">{n}</span><h3>{t}</h3><p>{c}</p></div>)}</div></div></section>
  <section class="section dark-section"><div class="shell mission-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">What changes as the trip develops</p><h2 class="section-title">Early flexibility becomes later certainty.</h2><p style="color:#aaa">A good process does not keep every option open forever. It deliberately narrows choices as the group gains information and commits.</p></div><div class="mission-stack"><div class="mission-card"><h3>Early planning</h3><p>Broad ranges are useful: destination options, likely dates, group size, budget, activity level, and support model.</p></div><div class="mission-card"><h3>Proposal stage</h3><p>Assumptions become more specific: route, lodging, suppliers, inclusions, payment model, minimum group, and cancellation terms.</p></div><div class="mission-card"><h3>Confirmed trip</h3><p>Traveler details, rooming, documents, equipment, flights, final schedules, support contacts, and supplier confirmations become the operating authority.</p></div></div></div></section>
  <section class="section"><div class="shell split-copy-image"><div><p class="eyebrow">Organizer communication</p><h2 class="section-title">Participants should not need to reverse-engineer the trip from email threads.</h2><p class="section-copy">As the plan becomes real, traveler information should become more consolidated. The organizer needs a clear way to explain what is decided, what is still open, what participants need to do, and when decisions are required.</p><div class="check-grid"><span>Trip overview and who it suits</span><span>Dates and likely travel windows</span><span>Activity and difficulty expectations</span><span>What is included and excluded</span><span>Payment and decision milestones</span><span>Rooming and traveler information</span><span>Preparation and equipment</span><span>Final itinerary and support contacts</span></div></div><div class="split-image" style={`background-image:url('${images.train}')`}></div></div></section>
  <section class="quote-strip"><div class="shell"><strong>The process should get quieter as departure gets closer.</strong><p>Travelers may still have questions, but the core plan should become more settled, more explicit, and easier to follow as the trip moves toward departure.</p></div></section>
  <PlanningCTA title="Start before you have solved every detail." copy="The first planning conversation is meant to establish fit and direction, not to test whether you already know how to build the trip yourself." />
</BaseLayout>
